Your syntax for variable interpolation is incorrect:

```diff
-"${COMPONENT}"
+"\(COMPONENT)"
```

You actually don't even need interpolation here, because `COMPONENT` is already a string. So it can just be `component = List(COMPONENT)`.

In case you aren't already, I highly recommend using one of the editor plugins for writing Pkl, because they will provide feedback about this to you. Pkl offers editor support including an IntelliJ Plugin (v0.35.1), VS Code Extension (v0.21.0), and Neovim Plugin (v0.6.0), all of which leverage the Pkl Language Server to provide IDE features like syntax highlighting and error detection.

Some other notes:

1. `List` is a data type that's meant for in-language operations (map/flatmap/filter/etc). If you want to describe _data_, you should use `Listing`.
2. If your module only makes sense as JSON, you should go ahead and set the `output.renderer`. Then, you don't need to add `-f json` anymore as a CLI flag.
    ```pkl
    output {
      renderer = new JsonRenderer {}
    }
    ```
3. I think it makes more sense to model your inputs as external properties instead of env vars.
    ```shell
    pkl eval -p SERVICE_NAME="$1" -p COMPONENT="$2" pkl-templates/my-template.pkl
    ```

    With this, you'd `read("prop:SERVICE_NAME")`
3. Instead of using `Dynamic`, try defining classes for your schema. Dynamic is sort of an escape hatch and has its uses, but generally you wouldn't use it for describing data. Also, try separating your data and schema into different files
    ```pkl
    // MySchema.pkl
    Tags: Tags

    class Tags {
      component: Listing<String>
    }

    output {
      renderer = new JsonRenderer {}
    }
    ```

    ```pkl
    // myData.pkl
    amends "MySchema.pkl"

    tags {
      component {
        read("prop:COMPONENT")
      }
    }
    ```

