```ts
import { env } from "bun";

const server = env.SERVER ?? "http://localhost:8090";
const userEmail = env.EMAIL ?? "user@example.com";
const userPassword = env.PASSWORD ?? "password";
const authToken = env.AUTH_TOKEN ?? await getHubAuthToken(userEmail, userPassword);

// may or may not be enabled
await getUniversalToken(authToken).then(console.log);

// make sure it's enabled
await enableUniversalToken(authToken).then(console.log);

// should be enabled now
await getUniversalToken(authToken).then(console.log);

// get user auth token
async function getHubAuthToken(userEmail: string, userPassword: string) {
   const response = await fetch(`${server}/api/collections/users/auth-with-password`, {
      method: "POST",
      headers: {
         "Content-Type": "application/json",
      },
      body: JSON.stringify({
         identity: userEmail,
         password: userPassword,
      }),
   });
   const { token } = (await response.json()) as { token: string };
   return token;
}

// get universal token
async function getUniversalToken(authToken: string) {
   const tokenResponse = await fetch(`${server}/api/beszel/universal-token`, {
      method: "GET",
      headers: {
         Authorization: authToken,
      },
   });
   return (await tokenResponse.json()) as { token: string, active: boolean };
}

// enable universal token
async function enableUniversalToken(authToken: string) {
   const tokenResponse = await fetch(`${server}/api/beszel/universal-token?enable=1`, {
      method: "GET",
      headers: {
         Authorization: authToken,
      },
   });
   return (await tokenResponse.json()) as { token: string, active: boolean };
}
```

Yeah, the `Zoned::offset()` method is the key. Bringing it all together:

```rust
use jiff::Zoned;

fn main() -> anyhow::Result<()> {
    let zdt = Zoned::now();
    println!("{zdt}");
    println!("{}", zdt.timestamp().display_with_offset(zdt.offset()));

    Ok(())
}
```

Has this output:

```
$ cargo r -q
2025-09-26T08:16:24.791183579-04:00[America/New_York]
2025-09-26T08:16:24.791183579-04:00
```

This is indeed intentional. Jiff follows the Temporal ISO 8601 grammar, which requires that datetimes prior to 1 BCE are represented with a leading `-` and 6 digits for the year.

Negative timestamps are somewhat odd and a bit of a grey area in ISO 8601 proper. According to ISO 8601, `-0280-01-01T00:00:00Z` is not inherently invalid, but requires "mutual agreement" among the parties to agree upon it, including a specification on the number of digits to allow for the year.

Note though that Jiff's `strptime` support is a bit more flexible. For example, this works:

```rust
use jiff::{civil::DateTime, tz::TimeZone};

fn main() -> anyhow::Result<()> {
    let s = "-0280-01-01T00:00:00Z";
    let dt = DateTime::strptime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", s)?;
    let ts = dt.to_zoned(TimeZone::UTC)?.timestamp();
    dbg!(ts);

    Ok(())
}
```

Or, if you want to specifically allow up to 6 digits for the year (it defaults to 4), then you can use `%06Y`:

```rust
use jiff::{civil::DateTime, tz::TimeZone};

fn main() -> anyhow::Result<()> {
    let s = "-000280-01-01T00:00:00Z";
    let dt = DateTime::strptime("%06Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", s)?;
    let ts = dt.to_zoned(TimeZone::UTC)?.timestamp();
    dbg!(ts);

    Ok(())
}
```

